The parts that needed care:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mfgets[0m semantics.[22m [38;5;10mchar in[100][0m means at most 99 bytes are read, the newline is retained, and reading stops there — a second line of input is ignored. The buffer starts as an empty C string, so immediate EOF leaves it empty and falls into the error branch. I read stdin byte-at-a-time from a locked handle (buffered by std) rather than using [38;5;10mread_line[0m, which would neither cap at 99 bytes nor stop at the right place.[0m[0m
