[0m[0m
[1mThe [38;5;10merrno[0m check.[22m [38;5;10mparse_val[0m requires [38;5;10merrno == 0[0m, and the only thing that sets it on this path is [38;5;10mERANGE[0m from overflow, so I track a [38;5;10mrange_error[0m flag and saturate to [38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m the way [38;5;10mstrtol[0m does. The magnitude is accumulated against the signed bound so [38;5;10m-9223372036854775808[0m stays representable. This check is actually redundant in the original — anything that overflows [38;5;10mlong[0m is already outside the [38;5;10mINT_MIN[0m/[38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m range — but I kept it structurally rather than simplifying it away.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mPreserved C overflow behavior.[22m [38;5;10mhouse->bedrooms += extra_bedrooms[0m is signed overflow, which is UB in C but wraps on the usual targets. Plain [38;5;10m+=[0m in Rust would panic in debug and I did not want release/debug to diverge, so I used [38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m. The [38;5;10m2147483647[0m test case exercises this and matches.[0m[0m
